1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,930
Pulse features. The first stage of every request, taken apart in depth.

2
00:00:05,730 --> 00:00:12,501
Features are the first stage of every request. They run before any filter, on every record in the cohort.

3
00:00:13,301 --> 00:00:21,565
Each feature adds new columns. A later feature can build on an earlier one, like a bucketize that reads the log column just made.

4
00:00:22,365 --> 00:00:29,136
And every later stage can read those columns, even the filters, which is something attributes can never offer.

5
00:00:29,936 --> 00:00:38,307
Pulse ships a growing set of feature operators, and more are added over time. Your instance's manifest always lists the ones it offers.

6
00:00:39,107 --> 00:00:46,582
Features come in two cost classes. Per-row features need only the record in front of them, so they finish in a single pass.

7
00:00:47,382 --> 00:00:56,243
Global-pass features need the whole cohort first: category counts, target means or quantile cut points. Pulse sweeps once to learn them.

8
00:00:57,043 --> 00:01:00,272
Then it rewinds and emits a value for every record.

9
00:01:01,072 --> 00:01:05,965
Both classes can stream. A global pass just costs a second read of the file.

10
00:01:06,765 --> 00:01:15,072
Many real measures have a long right tail: lots of small values and a few huge ones. That skew distorts averages and models.

11
00:01:15,872 --> 00:01:23,453
Square root is the gentle fix. A negative value becomes null rather than an error, so the rest of the cohort keeps going.

12
00:01:24,253 --> 00:01:33,093
Log goes further. Pulse uses the log of one plus x, so zero stays zero, and anything at or below minus one becomes null.

13
00:01:33,893 --> 00:01:41,496
Bucketize turns a number into a band. With explicit boundaries, each value lands in a numbered bucket, in a single pass.

14
00:01:42,296 --> 00:01:50,005
A value exactly on a cut point goes to the lower bucket. The output is the bucket number, not a label, and null stays null.

15
00:01:50,805 --> 00:02:00,093
Or ask for quantiles, and Pulse learns cut points that give every bucket the same number of records. You choose boundaries or quantiles, never both.

16
00:02:00,893 --> 00:02:08,410
Categories need encoding before a model can use them. One-hot gives every category its own zero-or-one column.

17
00:02:09,210 --> 00:02:16,727
The columns come from the schema's dictionary, so even a category with no records gets one. A null row is all zeros.

18
00:02:17,527 --> 00:02:26,431
Frequency encoding replaces each category with its share of the cohort. Features run before filters, so those shares count every record.

19
00:02:27,231 --> 00:02:32,679
Target encoding replaces a category with the average outcome of the records that share it.

20
00:02:33,479 --> 00:02:40,612
Smoothing pulls rare categories toward the overall mean. A category seen only twice shouldn't get an extreme score.

21
00:02:41,412 --> 00:02:51,020
But there's a trap. Each average includes every record's outcome, even test rows and the record's own. Pulse flags it every time with a leakage warning.

22
00:02:51,820 --> 00:03:00,276
A train-test split doesn't silence it. For training-only means, run a separate request on the training rows, and map the averages back.

23
00:03:01,076 --> 00:03:07,036
Date features split one date into calendar parts: year, month, day, day of week, and quarter.

24
00:03:07,836 --> 00:03:10,937
Day of week counts from zero, starting on Sunday.

25
00:03:11,737 --> 00:03:21,409
A datetime adds the hour, and is read on the local clock, so a time zone can move it to a different day. A plain date has no zone, so it never shifts.

26
00:03:22,209 --> 00:03:30,601
Polynomial features add powers of a number: its square, its cube, up to the tenth power. The original column stays as it is.

27
00:03:31,401 --> 00:03:39,516
A straight line misses a curve. Give a linear regression the squared column too, and it follows the bend. That's polynomial regression.

28
00:03:40,316 --> 00:03:46,447
Watch the scale. The tenth power of one hundred is ten to the twentieth. Standardise the column first.

29
00:03:47,247 --> 00:03:54,359
Train-test split tags every record with a partition: zero for train, one for validation, two for test.

30
00:03:55,159 --> 00:04:01,823
Stratify by a category, and each class is split in the same proportions, so the partitions stay balanced.

31
00:04:02,623 --> 00:04:10,631
The same seed and the same rows always give the same split. Downstream, a filter on split zero keeps just the training rows.

32
00:04:11,431 --> 00:04:18,884
Here's a full preparation pipeline in one request. Features run in the order you list them, so each can build on the last.

33
00:04:19,684 --> 00:04:28,076
The log of income feeds a decile bucketize. Region becomes indicator columns, and the signup date becomes calendar parts.

34
00:04:28,876 --> 00:04:36,329
Predict reports every new column before a single record is read, and flags the target encoder's leakage risk up front.

35
00:04:37,129 --> 00:04:45,286
That's the feature toolkit today: reshape a number, bin it, encode a category, split a date, add curvature, or partition the cohort.

36
00:04:46,086 --> 00:04:54,393
New feature operators are added over time, and embedders can register their own. The manifest always shows what your instance offers.

37
00:04:55,193 --> 00:05:00,385
Remember the one rule: features run first, so they see everything. Next up, filters.

38
00:05:01,185 --> 00:05:02,452
Pulse features.
